{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) sits at the vascular pole of the glomerulus, where the thick ascending limb touches its own parent glomerulus. It has the macula densa, the extraglomerular mesangium (lacis cells) and the juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole. It controls renin release and tubuloglomerular feedback, so it regulates blood pressure and filtration.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="PY7.2" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What is the juxtaglomerular apparatus?

The **juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA)** is a small structure at the **vascular pole of the glomerulus**. This is the point where the afferent arteriole enters the glomerulus and the efferent arteriole leaves it. Here the tubule of the nephron touches the arterioles of the glomerulus it came from. The part of the tubule involved is the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, close to the start of the distal convolution.

The JGA is a place where blood vessel and tubule are in direct contact, so the tubule can pass information to the vessel. It is a major structural part of the **renin-angiotensin system**, and it contributes to the control of glomerular arteriolar resistance and glomerular filtration.

## Components of the JGA

Standard references list the components slightly differently, but they agree on the same set of structures:

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/juxtaglomerular-apparatus/jga-structure.webp" alt="Labelled diagram of the juxtaglomerular apparatus." caption="The JGA sits at the vascular pole of the glomerulus." credit="OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juxtaglomerular_Apparatus_and_Glomerulus.jpg" width="960" height="356" /%}

1. **Macula densa:** a plaque of specialised cells in the wall of the thick ascending limb
2. **Extraglomerular mesangium:** also called the **lacis** or the **polar cushion**
3. **Terminal portion of the afferent arteriole:** it contains the renin-producing granular cells, also called **juxtaglomerular (JG) cells**
4. **Beginning (proximal portion) of the efferent arteriole**

Another way to group them is by type. The **vascular components** are the terminal afferent arteriole, the initial efferent arteriole and the extraglomerular mesangium. The **tubular component** is the macula densa.

The three specialised cell types of the JGA are the juxtaglomerular granular cells, the agranular extraglomerular mesangial (lacis) cells and the epithelial cells of the macula densa.

{% table %}
- **Component**
- **Where it is**
- **Key features**
---
- **Macula densa**
- In the wall of the thick ascending limb, where it touches the afferent arteriole and the extraglomerular mesangium
- Tall, columnar cells with large, tightly packed nuclei (hence the name). Sensors of the salt content of the tubular fluid
---
- **Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells**
- In the media of the terminal afferent arteriole, replacing smooth muscle cells; less often in the efferent arteriole
- Modified smooth muscle cells, also called epithelioid or myoepithelioid cells. Store renin in secretory granules
---
- **Extraglomerular mesangium (lacis cells)**
- Between the macula densa and the arterioles at the vascular pole
- Agranular cells forming a network that connects the sensory macula densa cells with the JG effector cells
---
- **Afferent and efferent arterioles**
- At the vascular pole of the glomerulus
- Their walls differ from usual arterioles in the JGA region because of the JG cells
{% /table %}

## Macula densa

The afferent arteriole and the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle lie in contact for a short distance. The **macula densa** is the patch of specialised cells in the wall of the tubule at this contact. The cells are tall and columnar and have large, tightly packed nuclei, which gives the "dense spot" its name.

Some structural points that help explain its job:

- The cells have large, oval nuclei and mitochondria concentrated at the apex.
- Unlike other parts of the thick ascending limb, the cells do not interdigitate with each other. They have a polygonal outline.
- The surface facing the tubular fluid is covered with short microvilli and carries one cilium.
- At their bases the cells have many infoldings, and their basement membrane blends with the matrix of the extraglomerular mesangium.
- A macula densa is a cluster of up to 40 tightly packed cells.
- Compared with other nephron epithelial cells, macula densa cells contain a high amount of nitric oxide synthase I and cyclooxygenase-2.

The macula densa cells are **chemoreceptors (osmoreceptors)** that monitor the sodium chloride content of the filtrate after it has passed through the loop of Henle. Chloride concentration in the fluid at the macula densa is the key signal.

## Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells

The **JG cells** are the source of the renin found in kidney extracts and in the bloodstream. They sit in the media of the afferent arterioles as the arterioles enter the glomeruli. They are large, rounded cells and have features of both smooth muscle cells and secretory epithelial cells. This is why they are called epithelioid or myoepithelial cells.

What they contain:

- **Secretory granules** that are membrane-bound and irregular in size and shape. **Renin**, their main product, is stored here.
- Small **protogranules** with a crystalline structure, which hold both the renin prosegment and mature renin.
- Myofilaments, a well-developed endoplasmic reticulum and many mitochondria.

Renin is released by **exocytosis** into the surrounding interstitium.

The granular cells are connected to the extraglomerular mesangial cells, to neighbouring smooth muscle cells and to endothelial cells by **gap junctions**. They are also **densely supplied by sympathetic nerve terminals**.

**Granular cells are modified smooth muscle cells.** When the body needs more renin, for example in volume depletion or narrowing of the renal artery, extra smooth muscle cells further upstream in the afferent arteriole wall can transform into granular cells. So the number of renin-producing cells is not fixed.

## Extraglomerular mesangium (lacis cells)

The extraglomerular mesangium is a network of star-shaped (stellate) cells, and the network is where the alternative name "lacis" (lace) comes from. These cells link the **macula densa sensory cells** with the **JG effector cells**, and it is likely that they carry the sensory signal across.

Two further points:

- The extraglomerular mesangium is bounded by the macula densa, the specialised regions of the afferent and efferent arterioles at the glomerular tuft, and the mesangial cells inside the tuft.
- It is **not vascularised**. Any paracrine signal released into it is not washed away by blood flow, so it can build up and act locally.

Renin has also been found in agranular lacis cells at the junction of the afferent and efferent arterioles, but the significance of renin in that location is unknown.

## Two functions of the JGA

The JGA does two different jobs. They seem to be kept strictly separate from each other.

1. **Local control of glomerular filtration (tubuloglomerular feedback):** it adjusts the flow resistance of the afferent arteriole of its own nephron.
2. **Control of renin synthesis and release:** the JGA is the major site of renin secretion, so it matters for the whole body.

For both, changes in the **chloride concentration of the tubular fluid at the macula densa** cause graded release of mediators. These mediators reach their targets by diffusion, which means they act in a **paracrine** fashion.

## Tubuloglomerular feedback

Because the thick ascending limb touches its own glomerulus, changes in the tubule can influence the behaviour of the neighbouring glomerulus. This is **tubuloglomerular feedback**.

- The macula densa senses the NaCl load in the filtrate.
- When the NaCl concentration changes, the feedback mechanism keeps an **inverse relationship between salt concentration and glomerular filtration rate**. A high salt signal at the macula densa reduces filtration, and a low one allows it to rise.
- Macula densa cells are thought to respond to a high salt concentration in the distal tubule by releasing **nitric oxide**, which dampens (inhibits) the feedback response.
- The role of the macula densa in stimulating renin release to raise filtration is less well understood.
- The vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole acts locally, adjusting the filtration of that one nephron.

The JGA therefore provides a feedback system that helps to maintain systemic arterial blood pressure when vascular volume falls and filtration falls.

## Renin and the renin-angiotensin system

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/juxtaglomerular-apparatus/jga-raas.webp" alt="Diagram of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system." caption="Renin release sets off the angiotensin cascade." credit="OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2712_Renin_Angiotensin_System-01.jpg" width="960" height="438" /%}

**Renin** is an enzyme. It acts on circulating **angiotensinogen** and starts the cascade that ends in **angiotensin II**. Angiotensin II then:

- raises blood pressure, and with it filtration rate
- stimulates the release of aldosterone and ADH
- increases sodium and water reabsorption, mainly from the distal tubule, which increases plasma volume

Renin release from the granular cells is the major source of systemic angiotensin II, so the JGA plays an essential part in controlling extracellular volume and blood pressure. The vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole, by contrast, serves a local role in modulating filtration of the nephron concerned.

## What controls renin release?

Renin secretion at any moment is set by the **summed activity** of several factors.

{% table %}
- **Factor**
- **How it acts**
- **Effect on renin release**
---
- **Intrarenal baroreceptor (JG cells as pressure sensors)**
- The JG cells sense stretch of the afferent arteriole wall, which reflects renal perfusion pressure
- Falls when arteriolar pressure rises; rises when pressure falls
---
- **Macula densa**
- Chemoreceptors that monitor the sodium and chloride load reaching the distal tubule
- Release is greatest when tubular sodium is low
---
- **Sympathetic nervous system**
- Circulating catecholamines and norepinephrine from renal sympathetic nerves act mainly on beta-1 adrenergic receptors of the JG cells, with a rise in intracellular cAMP
- Increases renin release, especially with upright posture
---
- **Angiotensin II**
- Acts directly on the JG cells (negative feedback)
- Inhibits renin release
---
- **Vasopressin**
- Inhibits renin secretion, although it is debated whether the effect in the living body is direct or indirect
- Decreases renin release
---
- **Potassium**
- The effect appears to be mediated through changes in sodium and chloride delivery to the macula densa
- Hypokalaemia increases renin release; hyperkalaemia decreases it
---
- **Atrial natriuretic peptides**
- Humoral factor
- Listed among the factors that influence renin release
{% /table %}

**Renin release is maximal** when renal perfusion pressure is low or tubular sodium is low, for example in renal artery stenosis, haemorrhage or volume depletion.

**Renin release is suppressed** by high perfusion pressure at the kidney, as in hypertension, and by a high-sodium diet.

Most of the conditions that raise renin secretion lower central venous pressure, which increases sympathetic activity. Some also lower renal arteriolar pressure. Constriction of the renal artery or of the aorta above the renal arteries lowers renal arteriolar pressure. Psychological stimuli increase the activity of the renal nerves.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Renin release, one line:** low pressure, low tubular sodium and high sympathetic activity push renin up. High pressure, a high-sodium diet and angiotensin II itself push it down.
{% /callout %}

## Why the arrangement is so useful

The JGA joins local and whole-body regulation in one small place. The macula densa samples the fluid that has just passed down the nephron. The lacis cells relay the message. The JG cells and the arteriolar walls respond, either by changing the resistance of the afferent arteriole or by releasing renin. This means each nephron can adjust its own filtration, while the same structure also adjusts blood pressure and extracellular volume for the whole body.

## Clinical correlates

**Renal or Goldblatt hypertension.** Constricting one renal artery causes a prompt rise in renin secretion and sustained hypertension. Renal artery stenosis lowers renal perfusion pressure, which is a strong stimulus for renin.

**Renin-secreting tumours (juxtaglomerular cell tumours).** These are rare tumours made of polygonal or spindle cells that contain cytoplasmic renin granules. Points to know:

- They are encapsulated and tan or grey-yellow, with scattered haemorrhages.
- The picture is hypertension with hypokalaemia, together with raised renin (and prorenin) and aldosterone levels.
- Hypertension can be severe, and proteinuria occurs in some patients. Glomerular filtration rate is normal or high.
- Blood pressure may fall substantially after the first dose of an ACE inhibitor or an angiotensin receptor blocker.
- On CT or MRI they usually appear as solid renal cortical masses, typically 2 to 4 cm in diameter.
- Unlike renal artery stenosis, blood flow to the affected kidney is not reduced, so the renal vein renin ratio between the two kidneys may not be raised.

**Abnormal renin-cell development.** Deleting a gene needed for processing regulatory RNAs in renin-expressing cells leads to far fewer JG cells, less renin and lower blood pressure. This shows how much normal kidney development and blood pressure depend on the renin cells.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Hypertension, hypokalaemia, high renin and high aldosterone together:** think of a renin-secreting tumour. Compare with renal artery stenosis, where renal blood flow to the affected kidney is reduced.
{% /callout %}

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Four parts of the JGA:** **M**acula densa, **E**xtraglomerular mesangium, **A**fferent arteriole terminal part (with JG cells), **E**fferent arteriole beginning. Remember: the macula densa is **tubular**, everything else is **vascular**.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Renin is stored in the secretory granules of the juxtaglomerular (granular) cells, which are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the terminal afferent arteriole." %}
Renin is produced and stored by:
- A. Macula densa cells
- B. Lacis cells
- C. Juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole
- D. Podocytes
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The macula densa is a plaque of specialised cells in the wall of the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, where it touches the afferent arteriole and the extraglomerular mesangium." %}
The macula densa is part of which segment of the nephron?
- A. Proximal convoluted tubule
- B. Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle
- C. Collecting duct
- D. Thin descending limb
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Renin release is maximal when renal perfusion pressure or tubular sodium is low, as in haemorrhage, volume depletion or renal artery stenosis. Hypertension and a high-sodium diet suppress it." %}
Which of the following would most strongly increase renin release?
- A. Haemorrhage
- B. A high-sodium diet
- C. Hyperkalaemia
- D. A rise in renal perfusion pressure
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Angiotensin II acts directly on the JG cells to inhibit renin secretion. This is negative feedback within the renin-angiotensin system." %}
Angiotensin II affects renin secretion by:
- A. Stimulating it through beta-1 receptors
- B. Stimulating it by raising intracellular cAMP
- C. Having no effect on it
- D. Inhibiting it by a direct action on the JG cells
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The extraglomerular mesangium is not vascularised, so a paracrine mediator released there is not washed away by blood flow. The macula densa signal can therefore build up and reach the effector cells." %}
Why is it useful that the extraglomerular mesangium is not vascularised?
- A. It allows renin to enter the blood faster
- B. A paracrine signal from the macula densa is not washed away by blood flow
- C. It prevents the JG cells from contracting
- D. It stops the macula densa from sensing chloride
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="A patient with hypertension, hypokalaemia and raised renin and aldosterone may have a renin-secreting tumour of the juxtaglomerular cells. Renal blood flow to the affected kidney is not reduced, unlike in renal artery stenosis." %}
A patient has hypertension, hypokalaemia and raised renin and aldosterone levels. Which is a likely cause?
- A. Primary adrenal failure
- B. A high-sodium diet
- C. A renin-secreting juxtaglomerular cell tumour
- D. Hyperkalaemia
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What are the components of the juxtaglomerular apparatus?" %}
The macula densa, the extraglomerular mesangium (lacis cells), the terminal portion of the afferent arteriole with its renin-producing granular (JG) cells, and the beginning of the efferent arteriole.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where is the juxtaglomerular apparatus located?" %}
At the vascular pole of the glomerulus, where the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle touches the afferent and efferent arterioles of its own parent glomerulus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What does the macula densa sense?" %}
The sodium chloride content of the tubular fluid after it has passed through the loop of Henle. Chloride concentration at the macula densa is the key signal.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is tubuloglomerular feedback?" %}
A mechanism in which changes in the salt concentration of the fluid at the macula densa change the resistance of the afferent arteriole of the same nephron, so that glomerular filtration rate moves in the opposite direction to salt concentration.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are the factors that control renin release?" %}
Stretch of the afferent arteriole (the JG cells acting as baroreceptors), sodium and chloride delivery to the macula densa, the sympathetic nervous system, and humoral factors such as potassium, angiotensin II, vasopressin and atrial natriuretic peptides.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the role of the lacis cells?" %}
They form a network that links the macula densa sensory cells with the JG effector cells, and they probably transmit the sensory signal between them.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which tumour secretes renin, and how does it present?" %}
A juxtaglomerular cell tumour. It presents with hypertension, hypokalaemia, and raised renin and aldosterone levels.
{% /viva %}

